ts for \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}= \\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points that must lie on at least one of the three lines.  \nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), undefined (vertical), and \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1. Slopes determined by pairs of points in \\(\\mathcal{P}\\)\n\nFor each unordered pair \\(\\{P,Q\\}\\subset\\mathcal{P}\\) compute the slope \\(m(P,Q)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\{P,Q\\} & m(P,Q)\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1)-(1,2) & \\infty \\\\\n(1,1)-(1,3) & \\infty \\\\\n(1,1)-(2,1) & 0\\\\\n(1,1)-(2,2) & 1\\\\\n(1,1)-(3,1) & 0\\\\\n(1,2)-(1,3) & \\infty\\\\\n(1,2)-(2,1) & -1\\\\\n(1,2)-(2,2) & 0\\\\\n(1,2)-(3,1) & -\\tfrac12\\\\\n(1,3)-(2,1) & -2\\\\\n(1,3)-(2,2) & -1\\\\\n(1,3)-(3,1) & -1\\\\\n(2,1)-(2,2) & \\infty\\\\\n(2,1)-(3,1) & 0\\\\\n(2,2)-(3,1) & -1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus the set of slopes that occur is  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{0,\\infty,-1,1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is sunny exactly when its slope belongs to  \n\n\\[\nS_{\\text{sunny}} = \\{1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Sunny lines that contain two points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\)\n\nFrom the table, the only pairs whose slope lies in \\(S_{\\text{sunny}}\\) are:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:\\; (1,1)\\text{ and }(2,2), &&\\text{slope }1,\\\\\nL_{2}&:\\; (1,2)\\text{ and }(3,1), &&\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\\\\nL_{3}&:\\; (1,3)\\text{ and }(2,1), &&\\text{slope }-2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nConsequently any sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\), and the only sunny lines that contain two points are precisely \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3. Attempt to use exactly two sunny lines\n\nAssume we have three distinct lines \\( \\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\) covering \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) and that exactly two of them are sunny.  \nLet the two sunny lines be a subset of \\(\\{L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\}\\).  \nWe examine the three possible choices.\n\