\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     L_1 &: x=1 \\quad(\\text{non\u2011sunny, but we are allowed to use sunny lines only; we replace it})\\\\\n     L_1'&: y=-2x+5,\\\\\n     L_2'&: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\\\\n     L_3'&: y=2x-1,\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n\n     all three have slopes \\(-2,-\\tfrac12,2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and together contain the six points of \\(S_3\\).\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume the statement holds for every integer \\(m\\) with \\(1\\le m<k\\) and \\(m\\neq2\\).  \n   Let \\(k\\ge4\\).  \n\n   - Consider the two points \\(P_1=(1,k)\\) and \\(P_2=(2,1)\\) of \\(S_k\\).  \n     Their slope is  \n\n     \\[\n     \\frac{1-k}{2-1}=1-k\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\qquad(\\text{because }k\\ge4).\n     \\]\n\n     Hence the line  \n\n     \\[\n     L_0:\\;y-(1)= (1-k)\\bigl(x-2\\bigr)\n     \\]\n\n     is sunny and contains exactly the two points \\(P_1,P_2\\).\n\n   - Delete \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) from \\(S_k\\).  \n     The remaining set is precisely \\(S_{k-1}\\): indeed, after removing the topmost point of column\u202f1 and the bottommost point of column\u202f2, each column \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le k-1)\\) now contains exactly \\(k-i\\) points, which is the definition of \\(S_{k-1}\\).\n\n   - By the induction hypothesis, \\(S_{k-1}\\) can be covered by \\(k-1\\) sunny lines.  \n     Adding the line \\(L_0\\) gives a family of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all of \\(S_k\\).\n\n   This completes the induction and proves that for every \\(m\\ge1,\\;m\\neq2\\) there exists a covering of \\(S_m\\) by \\(m\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Putting the pieces together.**  \n\n   - For any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq2\\) we use the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (non\u2011sunny) and then apply the construction of step\u202f4 to the remaining set \\(S_k\\).  \n   - The total number of lines is \\((n-k)+k=n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n\n   Suppose a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \n   Remove all non\u2011sunny lines; at most \\(n-2\\) of them can be removed, leaving at least three points of \\(S_n\\) uncovered (because the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) leave precisely the set \\(S_2\\), which consists of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\)).  \n\n   Any line that contains two of these three points is either vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\); all three possibilities are non\u2011sunny.  \n   Consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points, so two sunny lines can cover at most two of them \u2013 a contradiction.  \n   Hence no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nCombining the existence part (steps\u202f2\u20135) with the impossibility of \\(k=2\\) (step\u202f6) yields the complete description of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3,\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ and }k=2\\text{ is impossible}.}\n\\]"

