's sunny. That line contains exactly those two points. Then they remove those points, leaving S_{k-1}. They claim that after removing those two points, the remaining set is S_{k-1}. Let's verify: S_k consists of points (i,j) with 1 ≤ i ≤ k, 1 ≤ j ≤ k+1-i. Removing (1,k) (i=1, j=k) and (2,1) (i=2, j=1). The shape after removal: For i=1, we lose the topmost point (j=k). So column i=1 now has points j=1..k-1 (i.e., k-1 points). For i=2, we lose the bottommost point (j=1). So column i=2 now has points j=2..k-1? Wait, originally column i=2 has j from 1 to k-1 (since k+1-2 = k-1). Removing j=1 leaves j=2..k-1, which is k-2 points. For i≥3, column i originally has j from 1 to k+1-i. So after removal, columns i≥3 remain unchanged. So the shape after removal is not exactly S_{k-1} as defined? Let's check S_{k-1} = {(i,j) | 1 ≤ i ≤ k-1, 1 ≤ j ≤ (k-1)+1 - i = k - i}. So column i=1 has j=1..k-1 (k-1 points). Column i=2 has j=1..k-2 (k-2 points). Column i=3 has j=1..k-3, etc. So S_{k-1} has column i=2 with j from 1 to k-2. But after removal, column i=2 has j from 2 to k-1 (i.e., j=2..k-1). That's also k-2 points, but the set of j values is shifted up by 1: missing j=1, includes j=k-1 which S_{k-1} does not have (since for i=2, max j = k-2). So the shape after removal is not exactly S_{k-1} but is a translation? Let's examine more carefully.\n\nOriginal S_k points: For each i from 1 to k, j runs from 1 to k+1-i. So the shape is a right triangle with right angle at (1,1) and hypotenuse from (1,k) to (k,1). Removing (1,k) and (2,1) yields shape missing the top-left corner point and the bottom-left point? Actually (2,1) is near the bottom-left but not the bottommost point of the shape (the bottommost point is (k,1)). So after removal, the shape is missing a point at the top of column 1 and a point at the bottom of column 2. The shape is not exactly S_{k-1} but maybe can be transformed by a shear? However, the solution claims that after removal, the remaining set is precisely S_{k-1}